   "text": "The pattern is best explained by KCuO (mp-1103519) alone. I checked this with rough hand calculations, not a full simulation.\n\n**Observed peaks.** The main peaks sit near 13.25\u00b0, 18.4\u00b0, 27.0\u00b0, 31\u00b0, 32.1\u00b0, 33.0\u00b0 (strongest, 100), 38.4\u00b0, 43.5\u00b0 and 49.3\u00b0.\n\n**KCuO fit.** Reducing the primitive cell gives a body-centred tetragonal cell with a \u2248 9.39 \u00c5 and c \u2248 5.56 \u00c5. Its predicted reflections line up with the observed peaks:\n\n| Reflection | Predicted 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) | Observed 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 110 | 13.3 | 13.25 (weak) |\n| 101 | 18.5 | 18.4 |\n| 211 / 220 | 26.6\u201326.8 | 27.0 |\n| 002 | 32.2 | 32.1 |\n| 301 | 32.8 | 33.0 |\n| 321 | 38.1 | 38.4 |\n| 420 / 411 | about 43 | 43.5 |\n| 510 / 431 | 49.5 | 49.3 |\n\nThe 13.25\u00b0 and 18.4\u00b0 peaks are the strongest evidence, because no other candidate places lines there.\n\n**Other candidates rejected.**\n- **Fe3O4:** it would need its strongest line near 34.6\u00b0, which is absent.\n- **Li2Si2O5:** it would need strong lines at 24\u201325\u00b0, which are absent.\n- **Sr2CrMoO6:** it would give one strong line at 31.7\u00b0, not the observed 32.1/33.0\u00b0 doublet.\n- **Sr3Hf2O7 and KTmO2:** their low-angle and main lines fall at the wrong positions.\n- **BaSrSm(CuO2)3:** it could account for the 32\u201333\u00b0 doublet but not the 18.4\u00b0 or 27\u00b0 peaks.\n- **SrLa3MgAlO8:** I only roughly checked this perovskite-like cell. It could give a line near 32.8\u00b0, but nothing points to it as a second phase.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1103519\"], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"note\": \"The peaks at 13.3, 18.5, 27, 32.1/33.0, 38.4 and 49.3 degrees index well to body-centred tetragonal KCuO (a about 9.39 A, c about 5.56 A), and no other candidate fits these lines.\"}",
